<script>
  import iee754 from '../assets/ieee-754.svg?raw';
</script>

This implementation may look a bit peculiar if one is not very familiar with $64$-bit floats `f64`, which serve to encode the real numbers $\bbR$ with $64$ bits $(b_0, \ldots, b_{63}) \in \{0, 1\}^{64}$ like so.
$$
  \begin{aligned}
    f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) &= (-1)^{b_{63}} \Big( 1 + d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) \Big) 2^{E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) - 1023} \\
    E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) &= \sum_{i=0}^{10} b_{52+i} 2^i \\
    d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) &= \sum_{i=1}^{52} b_{52-i} 2^{-i}
  \end{aligned}
$$
This encoding of floating-point numbers $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) \in \bbR$ can be intuitively understood as partitioning the real numbers $\bbR$ first into diadic intervals $[2^n, 2^{n+1})$ and $(-2^{n+1}, -2^n]$ for integers $n$, choosing finitely many of these intervals ($12$ bits worth of them), and distributing $52$ bits of instances to each such interval.
The bits $b_{63}\cdots b_{0}$ determine the `f64` instance $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0})$ as such.

- The most significant bit $b_{63}$ determines the sign of the float $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0})$.
- The next $11$ most significant bits decide an _exponent_ integer value $0 \leq E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) \leq 2047$ which determines the integer scale $-1023 \leq n \leq 1024$ of the diadic interval $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) \in [2^n, 2^{n+1})$ or $f(b_{63}, \ldots, b_{0}) \in (-2^{n+1}, -2^n]$, depending on the sign.
  $$
    n = E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) - 1023
  $$
- The $52$ least significant bits decide the _fractional_ or _decimal_ part $d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) \in [1, 2)$, which serves to evenly distribute floats within the diadic interval.

<div>{@html iee754}</div>

This `f64` encoding can be thought of as distributing numbers uniformly within binary orders of magnitude: each diadic interval $[2^n, 2^{n+1})$ or $(-2^{n+1}, -2^n]$ has $2^{52}$ evenly distributed numbers.

Our implementation of `Uniform` is designed to sample $52$ bits with maximum entropy by leveraging `Rng::next_u64` and dropping the $12$ least-significant bits.
It uses these $52$ bits to generate an output $f(01\cdots1b_{63}\cdots b_{12})$ whose value is one of the uniformly distributed nodes within the dyadic interval $[1, 2)$.
This value is then scaled to produce $x \in [a, b]$.
$$
  x = \Big( f(01\cdots1b_{63} \cdots b_{12}) - 1 \Big) \times (b-a) + a
$$
The reason the implementation uses only $52$ bits is that this is the maximum amount of instances within `f64` whose values are uniformly spaced.
Note that large intervals $[a, b]$ with more than $52$ bits worth of `f64` instances will have "holes" (due to non-surjectivity), while small intervals $[a, b]$ with less than $52$ bits worth of `f64` instances will potentially have outputs of higher likelihood (due to non-injectivity).
Up to these minor headaches associated `f64`, it is fair to assume that our implementation of `Uniform` leverages the entropy of `Rng` to sample `f64` instances within $[a, b]$ of maximal entropy.
